window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"u":"index.html","a":"journey","t":"Solar System — explore the worlds of our Sun","s":"Eight worlds. One star.","k":"page","b":"A living, interactive map of the Solar System — scroll in toward Earth, or skip straight to the map. 8 planets 1 star Real NASA data Enter the map"},{"u":"index.html","a":"intro-title","t":"Solar System — explore the worlds of our Sun","s":"Explore the worlds of our Sun.","k":"page","b":"A living map of the Solar System, built as the way you navigate it. Focus any planet to preview it, open its page for real, sourced data — sizes, distances, moons, atmospheres and missions — and pick up facts worth keeping along the way. Enter the Solar System What is this?"},{"u":"index.html","a":"menu-title","t":"Solar System — explore the worlds of our Sun","s":"Choose a world to explore.","k":"page","b":"A live map of the Solar System. Focus any planet to preview it, then dive into the explorer or open its page for the real, sourced numbers. Explore the planets Meet the Sun Back to the Solar System Hover, tap, or tab a planet to preview it. Earth Terrestrial planet Open Earth →"},{"u":"index.html","a":"stats-title","t":"Solar System — explore the worlds of our Sun","s":"The system at a glance","k":"page","b":"Real numbers for the family of worlds orbiting our star."},{"u":"index.html","a":"you-title","t":"Solar System — explore the worlds of our Sun","s":"You, on another world","k":"page","b":"Gravity and orbits are abstract until they are yours. Enter a weight and an age and watch both come apart across the system. Your weight kg lb Your age Earth years"},{"u":"index.html","a":"missions-title","t":"Solar System — explore the worlds of our Sun","s":"Landmark missions","k":"page","b":"A few of the spacecraft that turned points of light into places."},{"u":"index.html","a":"outro-title","t":"Solar System — explore the worlds of our Sun","s":"Keep looking up.","k":"page","b":"Every value here comes from public NASA data — no invented numbers. The map is a model, not a photograph: orbit spacing and planet sizes are illustrative, not to true scale. Pick a planet and see what you can learn. Back to the map"},{"u":"formation.html","a":"","t":"How the Solar System formed — Solar System","s":"How it all began.","k":"article","b":"Nothing here was designed. A cold cloud of gas collapsed, spun into a disc, and swept itself into eight worlds — and we can date the whole thing to within about two million years, because the oldest rocks that fell to Earth still carry the clock."},{"u":"formation.html","a":"we-know-when-it-started-almost-exactly","t":"How the Solar System formed — Solar System","s":"We know when it started, almost exactly","k":"article","b":"The Solar System is 4.5682 ± 0.0016 billion years old. That number does not come from theory — it comes from lead-isotope dating of calcium–aluminium-rich inclusions, millimetre-scale white specks inside primitive meteorites. They were the first solids to condense out of the cooling nebula, so their crystallisation marks time zero. Every date on this page is counted from there. The Orion Nebula — a stellar nursery roughly 1,300 light-years away, and the closest thing we have to a photograph of our own beginning."},{"u":"formation.html","a":"the-nebular-hypothesis-stage-by-stage","t":"How the Solar System formed — Solar System","s":"The nebular hypothesis, stage by stage","k":"article","b":"The broad story is well established and has been since the eighteenth century in outline: a rotating cloud collapses, flattens, and accretes. What the last thirty years added is dates, and a good deal of argument about the middle chapters. Before time zero"},{"u":"formation.html","a":"a-cold-cloud-and-something-that-shoved-it","t":"How the Solar System formed — Solar System","s":"A cold cloud, and something that shoved it","k":"article","b":"The Sun formed inside a giant molecular cloud of hydrogen and helium, dense and cold enough that gravity could win. Meteorites contain the decay products of aluminium-26, an isotope with a half-life of only 717,000 years — far too short-lived to have been lying around. Something made it just before the Solar System formed, and the usual candidate is a nearby supernova whose shockwave also triggered the collapse. Caveat: Still debated An alternative holds that the aluminium-26 was produced locally, by cosmic-ray reactions near the young Sun, with no supernova required. First ~100,000 years"},{"u":"formation.html","a":"collapse-and-the-spin-that-flattened-everything","t":"How the Solar System formed — Solar System","s":"Collapse, and the spin that flattened everything","k":"article","b":"As the cloud fell inward it had to conserve angular momentum, so it spun faster — and a faster-spinning cloud cannot stay spherical. It flattened into a disc with a thickening knot at the centre. This is why the Solar System is flat: every planet still orbits in roughly the plane that collapse handed them, because they were built out of that disc. 0 – ~3 million years"},{"u":"formation.html","a":"the-protoplanetary-disc","t":"How the Solar System formed — Solar System","s":"The protoplanetary disc","k":"article","b":"Gas and dust settled into a rotating disc extending out past 100 AU, hot and metal-rich near the centre, cold and gas-dominated at the edges. Discs like this are short-lived: observations of other young stars put typical lifetimes at 1–10 million years, commonly around three, before the gas is blown away. Everything solid in the Solar System had to be assembled inside that window. ~1 million years"},{"u":"formation.html","a":"a-violent-young-sun","t":"How the Solar System formed — Solar System","s":"A violent young Sun","k":"article","b":"At the centre, the protosun contracted and entered its T-Tauri phase: wildly variable, accreting hard, and driving fierce stellar winds that helped strip the inner disc. It was not yet a star in the strict sense. Hydrogen fusion — the point at which the Sun joined the main sequence — did not begin until roughly 50 million years after time zero. Throughout"},{"u":"formation.html","a":"the-frost-line-decided-what-kind-of-world-you-ge","t":"How the Solar System formed — Solar System","s":"The frost line decided what kind of world you get","k":"article","b":"Somewhere near 2.7 AU — out in what is now the asteroid belt — lay the frost line: the boundary beyond which water, ammonia and methane could freeze. Inside it only rock and metal could condense, so the inner planets had little to build from and stayed small. Outside it, ice was abundant; cores grew fast enough to seize hydrogen and helium directly from the disc and became giants. The line was not fixed — it drifted as the disc cooled and its gas drained away. Where it settled is still legible: asteroids beyond about 2.7 AU are ice-rich, those inside dry. ~1.8 – 3 million years"},{"u":"formation.html","a":"dust-to-chondrules-to-planetesimals","t":"How the Solar System formed — Solar System","s":"Dust to chondrules to planetesimals","k":"article","b":"Dust grains stuck together electrostatically, then gravitationally. Chondrules — millimetre-scale beads of once-molten silicate, flash-heated to roughly 1,600–2,000 K and cooled again within hours — formed in this window and were swept straight into growing bodies. Kilometre-scale planetesimals assembled in as little as a few thousand years in the inner disc; the largest then ran away with the rest, reaching 100 km within a million years and Mars-sized embryos within about three. 10 – 100 million years"},{"u":"formation.html","a":"final-assembly-by-collision","t":"How the Solar System formed — Solar System","s":"Final assembly by collision","k":"article","b":"The last stage was not gentle. Dozens of planetary embryos on crossing orbits merged through giant impacts over tens of millions of years. The four terrestrial planets are what survived that demolition derby — and their spins, tilts and oddities are largely records of the last big thing that hit them. The impacts did not stop sharply either: the collision that made the Moon, below, may have come as late as 200 million years in. ~4.51 – 4.35 billion years ago"},{"u":"formation.html","a":"the-impact-that-made-the-moon","t":"How the Solar System formed — Solar System","s":"The impact that made the Moon","k":"article","b":"A body roughly the size of Mars, usually called Theia, struck the young Earth. The debris thrown into orbit accreted into the Moon — which is why the Moon is depleted in iron and matches Earth's isotopic composition so closely. Caveat: Age contested Dates from the hafnium–tungsten and calcium–magnesium systems span 4.51 to 4.35 billion years, and different minerals give different answers, so the timing remains open. Models, not measurements"},{"u":"formation.html","a":"did-the-giants-move","t":"How the Solar System formed — Solar System","s":"Did the giants move?","k":"article","b":"Two influential models say yes. The Grand Tack has Jupiter migrate inward to about 1.5 AU and then reverse outward with Saturn, truncating the inner disc and explaining why Mars and Mercury are so small. The Nice model adds a later instability, in which Jupiter and Saturn cross a resonance and scatter the outer planets into today's orbits. Caveat: Models Both reproduce features of the real Solar System well, but neither is established fact, and their timings are actively disputed. ~4.1 – 3.8 billion years ago?"},{"u":"formation.html","a":"the-bombardment-that-may-not-have-happened","t":"How the Solar System formed — Solar System","s":"The bombardment that may not have happened","k":"article","b":"Radiometric ages of lunar impact basins cluster suspiciously — Nectaris around 3.92, Imbrium around 3.85 billion years — which led to the idea of a Late Heavy Bombardment: a distinct, violent late spike in impacts. Caveat: Now questioned More recent work, including impact ages on Vesta running as early as 4.4 billion years, suggests bombardment peaked far earlier and simply declined, with no separate spike. The clustering may be an artefact of sampling only a few Apollo landing sites. A newborn star driving jets into its birth cloud — the same process that shaped the young Sun, caught around another star."},{"u":"formation.html","a":"discs-like-ours-around-other-stars","t":"How the Solar System formed — Solar System","s":"Discs like ours, around other stars","k":"article","b":"The strongest support for this whole account is that we can watch it happen elsewhere. Telescopes have imaged protoplanetary discs around young stars, complete with gaps and rings where planets appear to be sweeping their orbits clear, and have detected the organic molecules that rocky planets are built from still suspended in the dust. Life's starting materials detected in a dusty disc around a young star."},{"u":"formation.html","a":"the-record-is-still-on-the-moon","t":"How the Solar System formed — Solar System","s":"The record is still on the Moon","k":"article","b":"Earth erases its own history — plate tectonics, weather and water have recycled almost all of the original crust. The Moon does none of that. Its far side in particular preserves a nearly untouched record of four billion years of impacts, which is why lunar samples and orbital imagery remain the primary evidence for what the early Solar System was actually like. Poynting and Keeler craters on the lunar far side — an impact record Earth lost long ago. Back to the Solar System"},{"u":"exploration.html","a":"","t":"Fifty years beyond Earth — Solar System","s":"Fifty years beyond Earth.","k":"article","b":"Everything humanity has done off this planet fits into a single lifetime. Twelve people have walked on another world. Two of our machines have left the Sun's influence altogether. And someone has been living in orbit, continuously, since the year 2000."},{"u":"exploration.html","a":"the-numbers-before-the-stories","t":"Fifty years beyond Earth — Solar System","s":"The numbers, before the stories","k":"article","b":"Humans on the Moon 12 Across six landings, 1969–1972 Beyond low Earth orbit 24 Everyone who has ever left near-Earth space Years of continuous ISS crew 25 + Unbroken since 2 November 2000 Craft in interstellar space 2 Voyager 1 and Voyager 2"},{"u":"exploration.html","a":"opening-the-door-1957-to-1969","t":"Fifty years beyond Earth — Solar System","s":"Opening the door: 1957 to 1969","k":"article","b":"4 October 1957"},{"u":"exploration.html","a":"sputnik-1","t":"Fifty years beyond Earth — Solar System","s":"Sputnik 1","k":"article","b":"An 83.6 kg aluminium sphere with four antennas and a radio beacon. It did almost nothing except beep — and in doing so proved that orbit was reachable. 12 April 1961"},{"u":"exploration.html","a":"yuri-gagarin-one-orbit","t":"Fifty years beyond Earth — Solar System","s":"Yuri Gagarin, one orbit","k":"article","b":"108 minutes, a single circuit of the Earth aboard Vostok 1. Gagarin did not land in his capsule: he ejected at about 7 km altitude and parachuted down separately, as the design intended. 18 March 1965"},{"u":"exploration.html","a":"the-first-spacewalk","t":"Fifty years beyond Earth — Solar System","s":"The first spacewalk","k":"article","b":"Alexei Leonov left Voskhod 2 for 12 minutes and 9 seconds. His suit inflated in the vacuum until he could not fit back through the airlock, and he had to bleed off pressure to get inside. 20 July 1969"},{"u":"exploration.html","a":"apollo-11","t":"Fifty years beyond Earth — Solar System","s":"Apollo 11","k":"article","b":"Neil Armstrong, then Buzz Aldrin, on the Sea of Tranquillity. Eight years after Gagarin's single orbit. The lunar module Eagle on the surface, July 1969."},{"u":"exploration.html","a":"apollo-and-then-nothing","t":"Fifty years beyond Earth — Solar System","s":"Apollo, and then nothing","k":"article","b":"Six missions landed — Apollo 11, 12, 14, 15, 16 and 17. Apollo 8 and 10 flew to the Moon and came back without landing; Apollo 13 got there and survived not landing. Twelve people walked on the surface, all of them American, all of them men. The last was Apollo 17, in December 1972. Eugene Cernan was the last human to stand on another world, and Harrison Schmitt — the only professional geologist to go — was there with him. Nobody has been back since. In total, 24 people have travelled beyond low Earth orbit, all of them between 1968 and 1972. Earthrise, seen from the Orion spacecraft during Artemis — a deliberate echo of the Apollo 8 photograph that changed how we saw ourselves in 1968."},{"u":"exploration.html","a":"learning-to-stay-the-space-stations","t":"Fifty years beyond Earth — Solar System","s":"Learning to stay: the space stations","k":"article","b":"The story after Apollo is not about distance, it is about duration. The Soviet Salyut programme began in 1971 and worked out how to resupply and swap crews. Mir then held people continuously from September 1989 to August 1999 — 3,644 days. Valeri Polyakov's single stay of 437 days aboard Mir is still the record for one uninterrupted spaceflight. Assembly of the International Space Station began with the Zarya module in November 1998. Expedition 1 arrived on 2 November 2000, and there has been someone aboard every single day since — a run that passed Mir's record in 2010 and now stands at more than a quarter of a century. The International Space Station — continuously crewed since November 2000."},{"u":"exploration.html","a":"the-shuttle-thirty-years-and-two-losses","t":"Fifty years beyond Earth — Solar System","s":"The Shuttle: thirty years, and two losses","k":"article","b":"Between 1981 and 2011 the Space Shuttle flew 135 missions — Discovery 39, Atlantis 33, Columbia 28, Endeavour 25, Challenger 10. It built the ISS and launched and repaired Hubble. It also killed fourteen people. Challenger broke apart shortly after launch on 28 January 1986, when a booster O-ring failed in unusually cold conditions. Columbia disintegrated during re-entry on 1 February 2003. Seven crew were lost each time. Both accidents were traced to known risks that had been normalised over years of flights. Space Shuttle Discovery at launch."},{"u":"exploration.html","a":"the-robots-got-everywhere-first","t":"Fifty years beyond Earth — Solar System","s":"The robots got everywhere first","k":"article","b":"Every planet in the Solar System has been visited, and all of it was done by machines. First visit to each planet by spacecraft World First flyby First orbiter Mercury Mariner 10, 1974 MESSENGER, 2011 Venus Mariner 2, 1962 Pioneer Venus, 1978 (first US) Mars Mariner 4, July 1965 Mariner 9, 1971 Jupiter Pioneer 10, Dec 1973 Galileo, Dec 1995 Saturn Pioneer 11, Sept 1979 Cassini, July 2004 Uranus Voyager 2, Jan 1986 None — ever Neptune Voyager 2, Aug 1989 None — ever Uranus and Neptune have each been visited exactly once, by the same spacecraft, in a single flyby. Almost everything we know about the ice giants comes from those two encounters."},{"u":"exploration.html","a":"the-voyagers-left-entirely","t":"Fifty years beyond Earth — Solar System","s":"The Voyagers left entirely","k":"article","b":"Launched sixteen days apart in 1977 — Voyager 2 on 20 August, Voyager 1 on 5 September. Voyager 2 remains the only spacecraft to have visited all four giant planets. Both have now crossed the heliopause into interstellar space: Voyager 1 in August 2012, Voyager 2 in November 2018. They run on plutonium-238, which produced about 470 watts at launch and has decayed to roughly 220 watts. Instruments are being switched off one by one to stretch what is left. Each carries a gold-plated copper record holding 116 images, ninety minutes of music, and greetings in 55 languages — addressed to nobody in particular. The Pale Blue Dot. Earth photographed by Voyager 1 in 1990 from about six billion kilometres away — the single bright speck partway down the light band. Everyone who has ever lived is in this picture."},{"u":"exploration.html","a":"driving-on-mars","t":"Fifty years beyond Earth — Solar System","s":"Driving on Mars","k":"article","b":"Five NASA rovers have operated successfully on the Martian surface. Sojourner came first, riding down with Mars Pathfinder in 1997 and proving that the idea worked at all as it crept a few dozen metres around its lander. Spirit landed in January 2004 and worked until 2010 — 2,210 Martian days against a 90-day design life. Opportunity landed three weeks later and lasted until a planet-wide dust storm silenced it in June 2018, fifteen years into a three-month mission. Curiosity has been in Gale crater since August 2012 and is still working. Perseverance landed in Jezero crater in February 2021 and is caching samples for a future return mission. It carried Ingenuity, the first aircraft to fly on another world, which managed 72 flights before a damaged rotor ended its campaign in January 2024. Perseverance arrives at Mars, 18 February 2021."},{"u":"exploration.html","a":"bringing-pieces-home","t":"Fifty years beyond Earth — Solar System","s":"Bringing pieces home","k":"article","b":"Sample return is the hardest thing robotic exploration does. Japan's Hayabusa brought back grains from asteroid Itokawa in 2010, and Hayabusa2 returned material from Ryugu in December 2020. NASA's OSIRIS-REx delivered a capsule of asteroid Bennu to the Utah desert in September 2023. China's Chang'e missions have returned lunar samples, including the first ever taken from the Moon's far side."},{"u":"exploration.html","a":"what-happens-next","t":"Fifty years beyond Earth — Solar System","s":"What happens next","k":"article","b":"NASA's Artemis programme intends to put people back on the Moon, this time to stay, with commercial landers and a small lunar station in support. Schedules have slipped repeatedly and continue to move, so any specific date here would be out of date before you read it. The honest summary is that the hardware is being flown and tested now, and the return has not happened yet."},{"u":"exploration.html","a":"worth-sitting-with","t":"Fifty years beyond Earth — Solar System","s":"Worth sitting with","k":"article","b":"Roughly 600 people have been to space. Twenty-four have gone further than low Earth orbit, and the last of them came home in 1972 — before most people alive today were born. Back to the Solar System"},{"u":"instruments.html","a":"","t":"The instruments — Solar System","s":"The machines that saw it.","k":"article","b":"Every figure on this site — every temperature, every moon count, every ring — came from a specific piece of hardware built by people, launched on a rocket, and operated for decades. These are those machines, and the images they sent back."},{"u":"instruments.html","a":"telescopes-in-space","t":"The instruments — Solar System","s":"Telescopes in space","k":"article","b":"Putting a telescope in orbit buys two things: no atmosphere blurring the view, and access to wavelengths — infrared, ultraviolet, X-ray — that the air simply absorbs before they reach the ground."},{"u":"instruments.html","a":"hubble-space-telescope","t":"The instruments — Solar System","s":"Hubble Space Telescope","k":"article","b":"Operating Visible / UV / near-IR Launched by Shuttle Discovery in April 1990 and immediately found to have a mirror ground wrong by less than a fiftieth the width of a human hair — enough to ruin every image. A 1993 servicing mission fitted corrective optics, and Hubble went on to become the most productive telescope ever built. Launched 24 April 1990 Primary mirror 2.4 m Mass at launch 11,110 kg Orbit ~600 km, low Earth orbit Observations over 1.5 million An artist's concept, not a photograph. Hubble has been observing since 1990."},{"u":"instruments.html","a":"james-webb-space-telescope","t":"The instruments — Solar System","s":"James Webb Space Telescope","k":"article","b":"Operating Infrared Too big to launch assembled, Webb flew folded and unpacked itself over weeks — eighteen gold-coated beryllium hexagons and a five-layer sunshield, with hundreds of single-point failures and no possibility of repair. It sits at the Sun–Earth L2 point, 1.5 million km out, chilled to around 40 K so its own heat does not drown the infrared light it came to collect. Launched 25 December 2021 Primary mirror 6.5 m, 18 segments Mass ~6,200 kg Location Sun–Earth L2, 1.5 M km Operating temp. ~40 K (−233 °C) Webb's primary mirror, fully assembled before launch."},{"u":"instruments.html","a":"chandra-x-ray-observatory","t":"The instruments — Solar System","s":"Chandra X-ray Observatory","k":"article","b":"Operating X-ray X-rays cannot be focused by ordinary lenses or mirrors, so Chandra uses nested cylindrical mirrors that graze the incoming light into focus. It sees only the violent universe: matter falling into black holes, supernova remnants, and the hundred-million-degree gas that fills galaxy clusters. Its observations of the Bullet Cluster provided some of the most direct evidence that dark matter exists. Launched 23 July 1999 Length 13.8 m Mass 4,800 kg Resolution ~0.5 arcseconds Orbit apogee ~139,000 km Photographed at Kennedy Space Center, 1999."},{"u":"instruments.html","a":"spitzer-space-telescope","t":"The instruments — Solar System","s":"Spitzer Space Telescope","k":"article","b":"Retired 2020 Infrared A modest 0.85 m infrared telescope designed for two and a half years, which ran for sixteen. Spitzer pioneered the technique of measuring an exoplanet's atmosphere as it passes in front of its star, and quietly found an enormous, faint outer ring around Saturn that nobody had noticed in four centuries of looking. It was switched off on 30 January 2020; its data archive is still producing results. Operated 2003 – 2020 Primary mirror 0.85 m Wavelengths 3.6 – 160 µm Design life 2.5 years"},{"u":"instruments.html","a":"parker-solar-probe","t":"The instruments — Solar System","s":"Parker Solar Probe","k":"article","b":"Operating Solar corona The fastest object humans have ever built, and the closest anything of ours has come to a star. A 4.24 m carbon-composite heat shield takes the full force of the Sun at around 1,400 K while the instruments behind it sit near room temperature. It is flying through the corona to find out why the Sun's outer atmosphere is hundreds of times hotter than its surface. Launched 12 August 2018 Closest approach 6.1 million km Top speed ~192 km/s Shield front face ~1,400 K Dry mass ~559 kg Arriving at Kennedy Space Center for launch processing."},{"u":"instruments.html","a":"voyager-1-2","t":"The instruments — Solar System","s":"Voyager 1 & 2","k":"article","b":"Operating Interstellar Built with 1970s electronics and still returning data from beyond the Sun's influence. Between them they gave us our only close looks at Uranus and Neptune, discovered volcanoes on Io and the smooth ice shell of Europa, and are now measuring the interstellar medium directly. Their plutonium is running out; instruments are being retired one at a time to keep the rest alive. Launched 1977, 16 days apart Power at launch ~470 W Power now ~220 W Left the heliosphere 2012 · 2018 Golden Record 116 images, 55 languages Voyager 2's instrument layout."},{"u":"instruments.html","a":"cassini-huygens","t":"The instruments — Solar System","s":"Cassini–Huygens","k":"article","b":"Ended 2017 Saturn orbiter Thirteen years in orbit around Saturn and 294 circuits of the planet. Its radar found seas of liquid methane on Titan; ESA's Huygens probe landed there in January 2005, the only landing ever made in the outer Solar System. It also caught Enceladus venting water ice from a hidden ocean — which is why, when the fuel ran out, Cassini was deliberately flown into Saturn on 15 September 2017 rather than risk contaminating a moon that might host life. Launched 15 October 1997 In Saturn orbit July 2004 – Sept 2017 Orbits of Saturn 294 Huygens probe mass ~320 kg"},{"u":"instruments.html","a":"galileo-2","t":"The instruments — Solar System","s":"Galileo","k":"article","b":"Ended 2003 Jupiter orbiter The first spacecraft to orbit a gas giant, and the only one to drop a probe into Jupiter's atmosphere. Its magnetometer readings at Europa are the strongest evidence that a salty ocean lies beneath the ice — a finding so significant that Galileo was sent into Jupiter on 21 September 2003 to guarantee it could never crash into Europa carrying terrestrial microbes. Launched 18 October 1989 In Jupiter orbit Dec 1995 – Sept 2003 Dry mass ~2,223 kg"},{"u":"instruments.html","a":"and-the-ones-still-on-the-ground","t":"The instruments — Solar System","s":"And the ones still on the ground","k":"article","b":"Space is not always the answer. In the Atacama Desert, the Very Large Telescope runs four 8.2 m mirrors at 2,635 m altitude, each flexed by around 150 actuators to cancel out atmospheric distortion in real time — and able to combine into a single interferometer. Higher up at 5,000 m, ALMA operates 66 movable dishes as one radio instrument, spread from 150 m to 16 km apart. ALMA's images of gaps and rings in protoplanetary discs are the closest thing we have to watching planets being born."},{"u":"instruments.html","a":"what-they-showed-us","t":"The instruments — Solar System","s":"What they showed us","k":"article","b":"The same object, observed with different hardware, is a different object. Hubble sees dust as opaque; Webb sees straight through it to the stars forming inside. Neither picture is more real than the other. Hubble, visible light. The Pillars of Creation in the Eagle Nebula, first imaged in 1995 and revisited in high definition in 2015. The pillars are opaque towers of dust light-years tall. Webb, infrared. The Cosmic Cliffs in the Carina Nebula. At these wavelengths the dust turns translucent and hundreds of young stars appear that are invisible to Hubble. Cassini, from inside the system. Saturn's rings viewed from a vantage point no Earth-based telescope can reach."},{"u":"instruments.html","a":"why-this-page-matters","t":"The instruments — Solar System","s":"Why this page matters","k":"article","b":"None of the data on this site is obvious or self-evident. Someone had to build a machine, launch it, keep it alive for years, and interpret what came back. The numbers are real because the hardware was. Back to the Solar System"},{"u":"planet/mercury.html","a":"","t":"Mercury — Solar System","s":"Mercury","k":"planet","b":"Terrestrial planet · The swift, sun-scorched world · Known since antiquity The smallest planet and the closest to the Sun, Mercury races around its orbit while turning on its axis only slowly — so its day is far longer than its year. Back to the Solar System Next: Venus →"},{"u":"planet/mercury.html","a":"h-key","t":"Mercury — Solar System","s":"Key facts","k":"planet","b":"Distance from Sun 0.39 AU 57.9 million km Radius 2,440 km 0.38× Earth Mean temperature 167 °C −173 to 427 °C"},{"u":"planet/mercury.html","a":"h-time","t":"Mercury — Solar System","s":"Time & motion","k":"planet","b":"A day (one spin) 58.6 Earth days One day lasts about two-thirds of its year. A year (one orbit) 88 Earth days A year here is under three Earth months. Surface gravity vs Earth 3.7 m/s² 0.38 g 0 Earth = 1 g 2.5×"},{"u":"planet/mercury.html","a":"h-mass","t":"Mercury — Solar System","s":"Mass & physical characteristics","k":"planet","b":"Mass 3.30 × 10²³ kg 0.055× Earth Density 5.43 g/cm³ Water = 1.0 g/cm³ Escape velocity 4.3 km/s Speed to break free Orbital velocity 47.4 km/s Speed around the Sun Axial tilt 0.03° Almost perfectly upright — virtually no seasons."},{"u":"planet/mercury.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Mercury — Solar System","s":"Atmosphere","k":"planet","b":"Almost none — a trace exosphere of hydrogen, helium, oxygen, sodium and potassium. Effectively a vacuum, blasted away by the Sun. Surface pressure < 10⁻¹⁴ bar Relative to Earth's 1 bar"},{"u":"planet/mercury.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Mercury — Solar System","s":"Magnetic field","k":"planet","b":"Weak global field, about 1% the strength of Earth's."},{"u":"planet/mercury.html","a":"h-moons","t":"Mercury — Solar System","s":"Moons & rings","k":"planet","b":"Known moons 0 No known moons."},{"u":"planet/mercury.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Mercury — Solar System","s":"Composition","k":"planet","b":"Rocky world with a large iron core and almost no atmosphere."},{"u":"planet/mercury.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Mercury — Solar System","s":"Notable features","k":"planet","b":"Caloris Basin — a ~1,550 km impact basin ringed by mile-high mountains."},{"u":"planet/mercury.html","a":"h-facts","t":"Mercury — Solar System","s":"Fun facts","k":"planet","b":"Mercury has ice in permanently shadowed polar craters, despite being the closest planet to the Sun. Its axial tilt is near zero — the smallest of any planet — so Mercury has virtually no seasons. Surface temperatures swing from about −173 °C at night to 427 °C in daylight. One solar day on Mercury (sunrise to sunrise) lasts about 176 Earth days — two Mercury years. Its oversized iron core makes up roughly 60% of the planet's mass."},{"u":"planet/mercury.html","a":"h-missions","t":"Mercury — Solar System","s":"Exploration","k":"planet","b":"Mariner 10 1974–75 First flybys; mapped about 45% of the surface. MESSENGER 2011–15 First orbiter; found polar ice and mapped composition. BepiColombo 2018– ESA/JAXA mission arriving to orbit Mercury. Back to the Solar System"},{"u":"planet/venus.html","a":"","t":"Venus — Solar System","s":"Venus","k":"planet","b":"Terrestrial planet · The veiled furnace world · Known since antiquity Similar to Earth in size, Venus is wrapped in a thick carbon-dioxide atmosphere that traps heat, making it the hottest planet in the Solar System. Back to the Solar System Next: Earth →"},{"u":"planet/venus.html","a":"h-key","t":"Venus — Solar System","s":"Key facts","k":"planet","b":"Distance from Sun 0.72 AU 108.2 million km Radius 6,052 km 0.95× Earth Mean temperature 464 °C ~464 °C, nearly uniform"},{"u":"planet/venus.html","a":"h-time","t":"Venus — Solar System","s":"Time & motion","k":"planet","b":"A day (one spin) 243 Earth days (retrograde) Venus spins backwards, and slowest of all planets. A year (one orbit) 224.7 Earth days Its year is shorter than its day. Surface gravity vs Earth 8.87 m/s² 0.90 g 0 Earth = 1 g 2.5×"},{"u":"planet/venus.html","a":"h-mass","t":"Venus — Solar System","s":"Mass & physical characteristics","k":"planet","b":"Mass 4.87 × 10²⁴ kg 0.815× Earth Density 5.24 g/cm³ Water = 1.0 g/cm³ Escape velocity 10.4 km/s Speed to break free Orbital velocity 35.0 km/s Speed around the Sun Axial tilt 177.4° Tipped almost upside-down; it rotates retrograde."},{"u":"planet/venus.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Venus — Solar System","s":"Atmosphere","k":"planet","b":"96.5% carbon dioxide, 3.5% nitrogen, with clouds of sulphuric acid. A crushing, runaway greenhouse — like being 900 m under the ocean. Surface pressure ≈ 92× Earth Relative to Earth's 1 bar"},{"u":"planet/venus.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Venus — Solar System","s":"Magnetic field","k":"planet","b":"No global magnetic field."},{"u":"planet/venus.html","a":"h-moons","t":"Venus — Solar System","s":"Moons & rings","k":"planet","b":"Known moons 0 No known moons."},{"u":"planet/venus.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Venus — Solar System","s":"Composition","k":"planet","b":"Thick CO₂ atmosphere with sulphuric-acid clouds; a runaway greenhouse."},{"u":"planet/venus.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Venus — Solar System","s":"Notable features","k":"planet","b":"Maxwell Montes — its highest mountains, about 11 km tall. Volcanoes and lava plains have resurfaced almost the entire planet."},{"u":"planet/venus.html","a":"h-facts","t":"Venus — Solar System","s":"Fun facts","k":"planet","b":"A day on Venus (one spin) is longer than a Venusian year (one orbit around the Sun). Venus spins backwards — from its surface the Sun would rise in the west. Its surface pressure is about 92 times Earth's, enough to crush an unprotected craft. A runaway greenhouse keeps the surface near 464 °C — hotter than Mercury's. Bright sulphuric-acid clouds make Venus the most brilliant planet in our sky."},{"u":"planet/venus.html","a":"h-missions","t":"Venus — Solar System","s":"Exploration","k":"planet","b":"Pioneer Venus 1978–92 First US orbiter; early radar mapping. Magellan 1990–94 Radar-mapped 98% of the surface. Akatsuki 2015– JAXA orbiter studying the atmosphere and clouds. Back to the Solar System"},{"u":"planet/earth.html","a":"","t":"Earth — Solar System","s":"Earth","k":"planet","b":"Terrestrial planet · Our home world · The only known world with life The only place known to host life, Earth sits in the Sun's habitable zone with liquid water, a protective atmosphere, and a single large Moon. Back to the Solar System Next: Mars →"},{"u":"planet/earth.html","a":"h-key","t":"Earth — Solar System","s":"Key facts","k":"planet","b":"Distance from Sun 1.00 AU 149.6 million km Radius 6,371 km 1× (reference) Mean temperature 15 °C −88 to 58 °C"},{"u":"planet/earth.html","a":"h-time","t":"Earth — Solar System","s":"Time & motion","k":"planet","b":"A day (one spin) 23.9 hours The reference day for every comparison here. A year (one orbit) 365.2 days This is the reference year for the whole system. Surface gravity vs Earth 9.8 m/s² 1 g (reference) 0 Earth = 1 g 2.5×"},{"u":"planet/earth.html","a":"h-mass","t":"Earth — Solar System","s":"Mass & physical characteristics","k":"planet","b":"Mass 5.97 × 10²⁴ kg 1× (reference) Density 5.51 g/cm³ Water = 1.0 g/cm³ Escape velocity 11.2 km/s Speed to break free Orbital velocity 29.8 km/s Speed around the Sun Axial tilt 23.4° This tilt is what gives Earth its seasons."},{"u":"planet/earth.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Earth — Solar System","s":"Atmosphere","k":"planet","b":"78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen, 0.9% argon, ~0.04% carbon dioxide. The only known atmosphere rich in free oxygen — made by living things. Surface pressure 1 bar (reference) Relative to Earth's 1 bar"},{"u":"planet/earth.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Earth — Solar System","s":"Magnetic field","k":"planet","b":"A strong dipole field that shields the surface from the solar wind."},{"u":"planet/earth.html","a":"h-moons","t":"Earth — Solar System","s":"Moons & rings","k":"planet","b":"Known moons 1 One moon — the Moon. Notable moons The Moon"},{"u":"planet/earth.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Earth — Solar System","s":"Composition","k":"planet","b":"Nitrogen–oxygen atmosphere, liquid-water oceans, active plate tectonics."},{"u":"planet/earth.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Earth — Solar System","s":"Notable features","k":"planet","b":"The Mariana Trench (~11 km deep) and Mount Everest (~8.8 km tall) mark its extremes."},{"u":"planet/earth.html","a":"h-facts","t":"Earth — Solar System","s":"Fun facts","k":"planet","b":"Earth is the densest planet in the Solar System and the only one not named after a god. It is the only known world with liquid water on its surface — and with life. Its molten-iron core generates a magnetic field that shields us from the solar wind. The Moon drifts away from Earth by about 3.8 cm every year. Earth's oxygen-rich air is produced by photosynthetic life over billions of years."},{"u":"planet/earth.html","a":"h-missions","t":"Earth — Solar System","s":"Exploration","k":"planet","b":"Apollo 11–17 1969–72 Carried twelve astronauts to the Moon. International Space Station 1998– A continuously crewed orbital laboratory. Landsat 1972– Half a century of continuous Earth observation. Back to the Solar System"},{"u":"planet/mars.html","a":"","t":"Mars — Solar System","s":"Mars","k":"planet","b":"Terrestrial planet · The rusted frontier · Known since antiquity A cold desert world half the size of Earth, Mars carries dry riverbeds and the largest volcano in the Solar System — evidence of a more active past. Back to the Solar System Next: Jupiter →"},{"u":"planet/mars.html","a":"h-key","t":"Mars — Solar System","s":"Key facts","k":"planet","b":"Distance from Sun 1.52 AU 228.0 million km Radius 3,390 km 0.53× Earth Mean temperature −65 °C −140 to 30 °C"},{"u":"planet/mars.html","a":"h-time","t":"Mars — Solar System","s":"Time & motion","k":"planet","b":"A day (one spin) 24.6 hours A Martian day is remarkably close to Earth's. A year (one orbit) 687 Earth days (1.88 years) One Martian year is nearly two Earth years. Surface gravity vs Earth 3.7 m/s² 0.38 g 0 Earth = 1 g 2.5×"},{"u":"planet/mars.html","a":"h-mass","t":"Mars — Solar System","s":"Mass & physical characteristics","k":"planet","b":"Mass 6.42 × 10²³ kg 0.107× Earth Density 3.93 g/cm³ Water = 1.0 g/cm³ Escape velocity 5.0 km/s Speed to break free Orbital velocity 24.1 km/s Speed around the Sun Axial tilt 25.2° Close to Earth's tilt, so Mars has similar seasons."},{"u":"planet/mars.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Mars — Solar System","s":"Atmosphere","k":"planet","b":"95% carbon dioxide, 2.7% nitrogen, 1.6% argon. Far too thin to hold liquid water on the surface today. Surface pressure ≈ 0.6% of Earth's Relative to Earth's 1 bar"},{"u":"planet/mars.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Mars — Solar System","s":"Magnetic field","k":"planet","b":"No global field today — only patchy magnetism frozen into the crust."},{"u":"planet/mars.html","a":"h-moons","t":"Mars — Solar System","s":"Moons & rings","k":"planet","b":"Known moons 2 Two small moons: Phobos and Deimos. Notable moons Phobos Deimos"},{"u":"planet/mars.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Mars — Solar System","s":"Composition","k":"planet","b":"Thin CO₂ atmosphere; iron-oxide dust gives the surface its red colour."},{"u":"planet/mars.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Mars — Solar System","s":"Notable features","k":"planet","b":"Olympus Mons — the tallest volcano in the Solar System, about 22 km high. Valles Marineris — a canyon system more than 4,000 km long."},{"u":"planet/mars.html","a":"h-facts","t":"Mars — Solar System","s":"Fun facts","k":"planet","b":"Olympus Mons on Mars is about three times the height of Mount Everest. Valles Marineris is so long it would stretch across the United States. Mars has seasons like Earth because its axis is tilted about 25°. Its two tiny moons, Phobos and Deimos, were discovered only in 1877. Phobos orbits so close that it will break apart or crash into Mars within ~50 million years."},{"u":"planet/mars.html","a":"h-missions","t":"Mars — Solar System","s":"Exploration","k":"planet","b":"Viking 1 & 2 1976 First spacecraft to land and operate on Mars. Curiosity 2012– Nuclear-powered rover studying Gale Crater. Perseverance 2021– Caching samples; flew the Ingenuity helicopter. Back to the Solar System"},{"u":"planet/jupiter.html","a":"","t":"Jupiter — Solar System","s":"Jupiter","k":"planet","b":"Gas giant · The giant of the system · Known since antiquity The largest planet by far, Jupiter is a ball of hydrogen and helium with a centuries-old storm — the Great Red Spot — wider than Earth. Back to the Solar System Next: Saturn →"},{"u":"planet/jupiter.html","a":"h-key","t":"Jupiter — Solar System","s":"Key facts","k":"planet","b":"Distance from Sun 5.20 AU 778.5 million km Radius 69,911 km 11.0× Earth Mean temperature −110 °C cloud-top average"},{"u":"planet/jupiter.html","a":"h-time","t":"Jupiter — Solar System","s":"Time & motion","k":"planet","b":"A day (one spin) 9.9 hours The fastest spin of any planet, despite its size. A year (one orbit) 11.9 Earth years Jupiter takes almost 12 years to circle the Sun once. Surface gravity vs Earth 23.1 m/s² 2.36 g 0 Earth = 1 g 2.5×"},{"u":"planet/jupiter.html","a":"h-mass","t":"Jupiter — Solar System","s":"Mass & physical characteristics","k":"planet","b":"Mass 1.90 × 10²⁷ kg 317.8× Earth Density 1.33 g/cm³ Water = 1.0 g/cm³ Escape velocity 59.5 km/s Speed to break free Orbital velocity 13.1 km/s Speed around the Sun Axial tilt 3.1° Nearly upright, so Jupiter has little seasonal change."},{"u":"planet/jupiter.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Jupiter — Solar System","s":"Atmosphere","k":"planet","b":"About 90% hydrogen and 10% helium, with traces of methane and ammonia. No solid surface — banded clouds deepen into liquid metallic hydrogen."},{"u":"planet/jupiter.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Jupiter — Solar System","s":"Magnetic field","k":"planet","b":"The strongest planetary magnetic field, with intense radiation belts."},{"u":"planet/jupiter.html","a":"h-moons","t":"Jupiter — Solar System","s":"Moons & rings","k":"planet","b":"Known moons 115 115 confirmed moons, including the four large Galilean moons. Notable moons Io Europa Ganymede Callisto"},{"u":"planet/jupiter.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Jupiter — Solar System","s":"Rings","k":"planet","b":"A faint dust system — a main ring, an inner halo, and two wide gossamer rings."},{"u":"planet/jupiter.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Jupiter — Solar System","s":"Composition","k":"planet","b":"Mostly hydrogen and helium; no solid surface. Faint ring system."},{"u":"planet/jupiter.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Jupiter — Solar System","s":"Notable features","k":"planet","b":"The Great Red Spot — a storm wider than Earth that has raged for centuries."},{"u":"planet/jupiter.html","a":"h-facts","t":"Jupiter — Solar System","s":"Fun facts","k":"planet","b":"Jupiter is more than twice the mass of all the other planets combined. It has no solid surface — just deepening layers of hydrogen and helium. The Great Red Spot is a storm wider than Earth that has been observed for over 300 years. Jupiter spins fastest of any planet — a day there lasts under 10 hours. Its largest moon, Ganymede, is bigger than the planet Mercury."},{"u":"planet/jupiter.html","a":"h-missions","t":"Jupiter — Solar System","s":"Exploration","k":"planet","b":"Voyager 1 & 2 1979 Revealed the rings and volcanically active Io. Galileo 1995–2003 First orbiter; dropped a probe into the atmosphere. Juno 2016– Mapping the interior, gravity and magnetic field. Back to the Solar System"},{"u":"planet/saturn.html","a":"","t":"Saturn — Solar System","s":"Saturn","k":"planet","b":"Gas giant · The ringed jewel · Known since antiquity (rings first seen by Huygens, 1655) Famous for its brilliant rings of ice and rock, Saturn is the second-largest planet and the least dense — it would float in water if a bath were big enough. Back to the Solar System Next: Uranus →"},{"u":"planet/saturn.html","a":"h-key","t":"Saturn — Solar System","s":"Key facts","k":"planet","b":"Distance from Sun 9.54 AU 1.43 billion km Radius 58,232 km 9.1× Earth Mean temperature −140 °C cloud-top average"},{"u":"planet/saturn.html","a":"h-time","t":"Saturn — Solar System","s":"Time & motion","k":"planet","b":"A day (one spin) 10.7 hours Short days, like the other giants. A year (one orbit) 29.4 Earth years A single Saturnian year spans almost three Earth decades. Surface gravity vs Earth 9.0 m/s² 0.92 g 0 Earth = 1 g 2.5×"},{"u":"planet/saturn.html","a":"h-mass","t":"Saturn — Solar System","s":"Mass & physical characteristics","k":"planet","b":"Mass 5.68 × 10²⁶ kg 95.2× Earth Density 0.69 g/cm³ Water = 1.0 g/cm³ Escape velocity 35.5 km/s Speed to break free Orbital velocity 9.7 km/s Speed around the Sun Axial tilt 26.7° An Earth-like tilt that tips its rings toward and away from us."},{"u":"planet/saturn.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Saturn — Solar System","s":"Atmosphere","k":"planet","b":"About 96% hydrogen and 3% helium, with traces of methane and ammonia. Fierce equatorial winds reach roughly 1,800 km/h."},{"u":"planet/saturn.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Saturn — Solar System","s":"Magnetic field","k":"planet","b":"A strong field, unusually well aligned with the planet's rotation axis."},{"u":"planet/saturn.html","a":"h-moons","t":"Saturn — Solar System","s":"Moons & rings","k":"planet","b":"Known moons 293 293 confirmed moons — the most of any planet, including Titan. Notable moons Titan Enceladus Rhea Iapetus Mimas"},{"u":"planet/saturn.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Saturn — Solar System","s":"Rings","k":"planet","b":"The brightest rings in the Solar System — mostly water ice, split by the Cassini Division."},{"u":"planet/saturn.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Saturn — Solar System","s":"Composition","k":"planet","b":"Hydrogen and helium; spectacular rings of ice, dust and rock."},{"u":"planet/saturn.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Saturn — Solar System","s":"Notable features","k":"planet","b":"The bright ring system, spanning about 280,000 km yet only tens of metres thick. A six-sided polar jet stream — Saturn's 'hexagon'."},{"u":"planet/saturn.html","a":"h-facts","t":"Saturn — Solar System","s":"Fun facts","k":"planet","b":"Saturn is the least dense planet — it would float in water if you had a big enough bath. Its rings are made mostly of water ice and may be only 100–200 million years old. The moon Titan has a thick atmosphere and lakes of liquid methane. Enceladus shoots geysers of water ice into space from a hidden ocean. A persistent six-sided jet stream circles Saturn's north pole."},{"u":"planet/saturn.html","a":"h-missions","t":"Saturn — Solar System","s":"Exploration","k":"planet","b":"Pioneer 11 1979 First spacecraft to fly past Saturn. Voyager 1 & 2 1980–81 Detailed the rings and hazy Titan. Cassini–Huygens 2004–17 Orbited Saturn; landed a probe on Titan. Back to the Solar System"},{"u":"planet/uranus.html","a":"","t":"Uranus — Solar System","s":"Uranus","k":"planet","b":"Ice giant · The tilted ice giant · William Herschel, 1781 — the first planet found with a telescope An icy world of water, methane and ammonia, Uranus is tipped almost completely on its side, so it appears to roll along its orbit. Back to the Solar System Next: Neptune →"},{"u":"planet/uranus.html","a":"h-key","t":"Uranus — Solar System","s":"Key facts","k":"planet","b":"Distance from Sun 19.19 AU 2.87 billion km Radius 25,362 km 4.0× Earth Mean temperature −195 °C coldest planetary atmosphere"},{"u":"planet/uranus.html","a":"h-time","t":"Uranus — Solar System","s":"Time & motion","k":"planet","b":"A day (one spin) 17.2 hours (retrograde) Uranus spins on its side, tipped about 98°. A year (one orbit) 84 Earth years It has completed only a couple of orbits since its 1781 discovery. Surface gravity vs Earth 8.7 m/s² 0.89 g 0 Earth = 1 g 2.5×"},{"u":"planet/uranus.html","a":"h-mass","t":"Uranus — Solar System","s":"Mass & physical characteristics","k":"planet","b":"Mass 8.68 × 10²⁵ kg 14.5× Earth Density 1.27 g/cm³ Water = 1.0 g/cm³ Escape velocity 21.3 km/s Speed to break free Orbital velocity 6.8 km/s Speed around the Sun Axial tilt 97.8° Tipped past 90° — Uranus rolls along its orbit on its side."},{"u":"planet/uranus.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Uranus — Solar System","s":"Atmosphere","k":"planet","b":"About 83% hydrogen, 15% helium and 2% methane. Methane absorbs red light, giving Uranus its pale cyan colour."},{"u":"planet/uranus.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Uranus — Solar System","s":"Magnetic field","k":"planet","b":"A lopsided field, tilted 59° from the axis and offset from the planet's centre."},{"u":"planet/uranus.html","a":"h-moons","t":"Uranus — Solar System","s":"Moons & rings","k":"planet","b":"Known moons 29 29 confirmed moons, named after literary characters. Notable moons Titania Oberon Umbriel Ariel Miranda"},{"u":"planet/uranus.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Uranus — Solar System","s":"Rings","k":"planet","b":"Thirteen narrow, dark rings of rocky and icy debris — faint and hard to see."},{"u":"planet/uranus.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Uranus — Solar System","s":"Composition","k":"planet","b":"Water, methane and ammonia ices over a small rocky core; methane tints it blue-green."},{"u":"planet/uranus.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Uranus — Solar System","s":"Notable features","k":"planet","b":"Miranda — a moon of chasms and cliffs more than 10 km high."},{"u":"planet/uranus.html","a":"h-facts","t":"Uranus — Solar System","s":"Fun facts","k":"planet","b":"Uranus's extreme tilt gives each pole roughly 42 years of continuous sunlight, then 42 of darkness. It rotates almost on its side, as if rolling along its orbit. Uranus was the first planet discovered with a telescope, by William Herschel in 1781. Methane in its air absorbs red light, giving the planet its cyan colour. Its magnetic field is tilted 59° from its axis and offset from the planet's centre."},{"u":"planet/uranus.html","a":"h-missions","t":"Uranus — Solar System","s":"Exploration","k":"planet","b":"Voyager 2 1986 The only spacecraft to visit — found 10 new moons. James Webb Telescope 2023– Imaged its rings and auroras from afar. Back to the Solar System"},{"u":"planet/neptune.html","a":"","t":"Neptune — Solar System","s":"Neptune","k":"planet","b":"Ice giant · The windswept deep · Johann Galle, 1846 — found within 1° of Le Verrier's mathematical prediction The most distant planet, Neptune is a deep-blue ice giant with the fastest winds in the Solar System, found by mathematics before it was ever seen. Back to the Solar System Next: Mercury →"},{"u":"planet/neptune.html","a":"h-key","t":"Neptune — Solar System","s":"Key facts","k":"planet","b":"Distance from Sun 30.07 AU 4.50 billion km Radius 24,622 km 3.9× Earth Mean temperature −200 °C cloud-top average"},{"u":"planet/neptune.html","a":"h-time","t":"Neptune — Solar System","s":"Time & motion","k":"planet","b":"A day (one spin) 16.1 hours A short day for such a distant world. A year (one orbit) 164.8 Earth years Neptune completed its first orbit since discovery only in 2011. Surface gravity vs Earth 11.0 m/s² 1.12 g 0 Earth = 1 g 2.5×"},{"u":"planet/neptune.html","a":"h-mass","t":"Neptune — Solar System","s":"Mass & physical characteristics","k":"planet","b":"Mass 1.02 × 10²⁶ kg 17.1× Earth Density 1.64 g/cm³ Water = 1.0 g/cm³ Escape velocity 23.5 km/s Speed to break free Orbital velocity 5.4 km/s Speed around the Sun Axial tilt 28.3° An Earth-like tilt, so Neptune has seasons — each lasting ~40 years."},{"u":"planet/neptune.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Neptune — Solar System","s":"Atmosphere","k":"planet","b":"About 80% hydrogen, 19% helium and 1.5% methane. Methane deepens its blue; winds top 2,000 km/h — the fastest of any planet."},{"u":"planet/neptune.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Neptune — Solar System","s":"Magnetic field","k":"planet","b":"A tilted, off-centre field, inclined about 47° from the rotation axis."},{"u":"planet/neptune.html","a":"h-moons","t":"Neptune — Solar System","s":"Moons & rings","k":"planet","b":"Known moons 16 16 confirmed moons, the largest being Triton. Notable moons Triton Proteus Nereid"},{"u":"planet/neptune.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Neptune — Solar System","s":"Rings","k":"planet","b":"Five faint rings of dark dust, named after astronomers such as Adams and Galle."},{"u":"planet/neptune.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Neptune — Solar System","s":"Composition","k":"planet","b":"Water, methane and ammonia ices; methane gives its vivid blue colour."},{"u":"planet/neptune.html","a":"dashboard","t":"Neptune — Solar System","s":"Notable features","k":"planet","b":"The Great Dark Spot — an Earth-sized storm seen by Voyager 2 in 1989."},{"u":"planet/neptune.html","a":"h-facts","t":"Neptune — Solar System","s":"Fun facts","k":"planet","b":"Neptune was predicted by mathematics from Uranus's orbit, then found within a degree. It has the fastest winds in the Solar System — over 2,000 km/h. Neptune radiates more heat than it receives from the distant Sun. Its moon Triton orbits backwards and has active nitrogen geysers. Neptune completed its first orbit since discovery only in 2011."},{"u":"planet/neptune.html","a":"h-missions","t":"Neptune — Solar System","s":"Exploration","k":"planet","b":"Voyager 2 1989 The only visit — revealed the Great Dark Spot and Triton's geysers. Hubble Telescope 1994– Tracks the birth and death of its giant storms. Back to the Solar System"}];
